      {
        "id": "lem-covered-edge-reversal-preserves-d-separation",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Covered edge reversal preserves d-separation",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "deps": [
          "def-ancestral-moral-graph-of-a-dag",
          "lem-d-separation-ancestral-moral-separation"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "statement": "Let $G$ be a finite DAG and let $x\\to y$ be a covered arrow, meaning\n$\\operatorname{pa}_G(y)=\\operatorname{pa}_G(x)\\cup\\{x\\}$. Replacing $x\\to y$\nby $y\\to x$ gives a DAG $G'$ with exactly the same d-separation relations as\n$G$."
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-chordal-components-admit-both-orientations",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Edges of finite chordal graphs admit both collider-free orientations",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "deps": [],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "statement": "An undirected graph is **chordal** when it has no induced cycle of length at\nleast four. A vertex is **simplicial** when all of its neighbors are pairwise\nadjacent. A **perfect elimination order** is a total order in which the later\nneighbors of each vertex form a clique. For every finite chordal undirected\ngraph $H$ and every edge $\\{u,v\\}$ of $H$, there are acyclic orientations of\nall edges of $H$ with no unshielded collider $a\\to w\\leftarrow b$ with\n$a,b$ nonadjacent, one having $u\\to v$ and another having $v\\to u$."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-gaussian-partial-correlation",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Gaussian partial correlation",
        "strategy": "definition",
        "deps": [
          "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "def-axiom-of-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "justified_by": [
          "thm-gaussian-zero-partial-correlation-is-conditional-independence"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume AC. Let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_p)$ have a nondegenerate multivariate-normal\nlaw with mean $\\mu$ and positive-definite covariance matrix $\\Sigma$. For\ndistinct indices $i,j$ and $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,p\\}\\setminus\\{i,j\\}$, define\nthe conditional covariance block\n$$\nK_{ij\\mid S}=\\Sigma_{\\{i,j\\},\\{i,j\\}}-\\Sigma_{\\{i,j\\},S}\\Sigma_{S,S}^{-1}\\Sigma_{S,\\{i,j\\}},\n$$\nwith $K_{ij\\mid\\varnothing}=\\Sigma_{\\{i,j\\},\\{i,j\\}}$. Whenever the diagonal\nentries of this block are positive, the **partial correlation** is\n$$\n\\rho_{ij\\mid S}=\\frac{(K_{ij\\mid S})_{12}}{\\sqrt{(K_{ij\\mid S})_{11}(K_{ij\\mid S})_{22}}}.\n$$\nThe nondegenerate Gaussian case makes the conditioning covariance block\ninvertible; the following theorem proves that $K_{ij\\mid S}$ is positive\ndefinite, so this definition applies to every such $i,j,S$."
      },
      {
